infall continues.\n\n* WHERE...Western Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern Hinsdale County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 400 PM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is occurring. Streams levels remain\nelevated due to excess runoff from earlier rainfall. Multiple road\nclosures are in effect county wide.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 147 PM MDT, local law enforcement reported heavy rain in the\nwarned area since Thursday. Flooding is ongoing in the warned\narea. Rivers are forecast to slowly recede throughout this\nafternoon\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nLightner Creek, Grimes Creek, Salt Creek, Lake Creek, Castle\nCreek, Florida River, East Creek, Long Hollow, McDermott Arroyo,\nJohnny Pond Arroyo, Fall Creek, Junction Creek, Little Bear\nCreek, Taylor Creek, Vallecito Creek, Red Creek, Tirbircio Creek,\nTrue Creek, South Fork Texas Creek, North Fork Texas Creek,\nAnimas River, Beaver Creek, Falls Creek, First Fork Red Creek,\nFirst Fork Piedra River, Bear Creek, Hay Gulch, Deadwood Creek, D\nCreek, Plata River, La, San Juan Arroyo, Mud Spring Creek,\nShearer Creek, Basin Creek, Pinos River, Los, Cherry Creek,\nSpring Creek, Wheeler Arroyo, Dry Creek, South Fork Lightner\nCreek, West Fork Shearer Creek and Weasel Skin Creek.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, La Plata, Falfa, Hesperus, Bondad, Breen, Mayday, Kline,\nMarvel, Redmesa and Vallecito.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T16:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7aff6e7b499588ea45403ba63f4221c2775e9daa.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7aff6e7b499588ea45403ba63f4221c2775e9daa.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 23 at 11:59AM MDT until October 25 at 12:00PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and Utah,\nincluding the following areas, Arches/Grand Flat,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through Sunday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recent burn scars and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Flooding is imminent or ongoing with additional rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms expected through Saturday afternoon. Rivers and\ncreeks may still experience high waters even after rainfall has\nended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-25T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T20:04:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 232004 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 204 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding is ongoing across portions of southeast Utah and southwest Colorado, and is expected to continue through Saturday. - Many rivers will experience their crest Friday or Saturday. - Isolat